WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.760
A virtual machine pretends to be a whole computer.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:10.777
It has virtual hardware, and it boots a full operating system with its own kernel.

3
00:00:11.000 --> 00:00:13.976
The kernel is the core of an operating system.

4
00:00:13.976 --> 00:00:18.181
It starts processes, hands out memory, and talks to the hardware.

5
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:20.978
Booting all of that takes many seconds.

6
00:00:20.978 --> 00:00:24.152
And each VM holds on to its own share of memory.

7
00:00:24.967 --> 00:00:27.016
A container has none of that.

8
00:00:27.016 --> 00:00:32.105
It is a normal Linux process, the same kind you listed with ps on Day 6.

9
00:00:32.333 --> 00:00:34.601
The kernel gives it two extra things.

10
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:42.264
Namespaces give the process a private view of the system: its own process list, network, hostname and files.

11
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:47.103
They work like the blinkers on a horse, the flaps that stop it seeing to the side.

12
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:53.015
cgroups, short for control groups, give it a budget: a cap on CPU and memory.

13
00:00:53.233 --> 00:00:55.885
Past the memory cap, the kernel kills it.

14
00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:01.798
You can watch namespaces work with Alpine, a tiny Linux image.

15
00:01:01.798 --> 00:01:03.087
Run ps inside it.

16
00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:10.923
It lists a single process: ps itself, running as PID 1, the number a system gives its first process.

17
00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:15.599
The machine runs hundreds of processes, and the container sees none of them.

18
00:01:15.800 --> 00:01:20.052
In the same way, listing the root folder shows Alpine's own folders.

19
00:01:20.267 --> 00:01:22.300
Those files come from the image.

20
00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:24.397
None of the host's files show up.

21
00:01:25.300 --> 00:01:29.531
Put together, docker run nginx does five things, in this order.

22
00:01:29.733 --> 00:01:30.028
One.

23
00:01:30.028 --> 00:01:36.445
The docker command on your Mac sends the request to Docker Engine, inside the Linux VM.

24
00:01:37.467 --> 00:01:37.735
Two.

25
00:01:37.735 --> 00:01:43.773
The engine checks whether it already has the image, and pulls it from the registry if not.

26
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:44.401
Three.

27
00:01:44.401 --> 00:01:48.348
It puts a fresh writable layer on top of the image's files.

28
00:01:48.348 --> 00:01:52.361
That merged folder tree becomes the container's root folder.

29
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:52.959
Four.

30
00:01:52.959 --> 00:01:57.039
It asks the kernel for new namespaces, and a cgroup.

31
00:01:57.267 --> 00:01:57.687
Five.

32
00:01:57.687 --> 00:02:02.730
It starts the image's default command inside them, as PID 1.

33
00:02:02.730 --> 00:02:05.756
For nginx, that is the nginx server.

34
00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:11.520
The engine hands steps three to five to two helper programs, containerd and runc.

35
00:02:12.733 --> 00:02:15.552
Now compare the start of a virtual machine.

36
00:02:15.552 --> 00:02:20.993
Virtual hardware, its own kernel, a whole operating system, and only then your app.

37
00:02:20.993 --> 00:02:22.567
That takes many seconds.

38
00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.829
A container skips all of that.

39
00:02:24.829 --> 00:02:26.791
No second kernel has to boot.

40
00:02:26.791 --> 00:02:31.119
Docker builds nothing new: it only puts fences around a process.

41
00:02:31.119 --> 00:02:33.216
So it starts in about a second.

42
00:02:34.033 --> 00:02:36.233
You pay for that speed by sharing.

43
00:02:36.233 --> 00:02:39.273
Every container on a host uses the same kernel.

44
00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:41.896
So a kernel bug can affect all of them.

45
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:46.992
And a container can only run programs built for that kernel, and that CPU.

46
00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.548
So a container is an ordinary Linux process, started from an image's files.

47
00:02:52.548 --> 00:02:56.347
Namespaces give it its own view, and cgroups give it limits.
